CE 68.41”, “FREE DANCE 110.42”, “TOTAL 221.57” — which matches their **2014 Olympic Free Dance total** (they won gold with 221.57). The backdrop also has a different design, consistent with Sochi 2014.\n  - **PyeongChang 2018 is NOT visible.** None of the frames show branding, score overlays, or backdrops specific to PyeongChang 2018. The video does **not** include footage from their third Olympic Games.\n\n#### 4) **Multiple different Olympic settings/backdrops?**\n- **YES.** There are at least two distinct Olympic backdrops:\n  - **Vancouver 2010** (light blue with white rings and snowflake patterns — Frames 13–23).\n  - **Sochi 2014** (blue with abstract wave patterns — Frames 24–30).\n  - **No PyeongChang 2018 backdrop is visible.**\n\n#### 5) **Medal ceremonies/award presentations?**\n- **NO.** While there are emotional moments of celebration and hugging (Frames 22–23, 26–30), **no actual medal ceremony** (e.g., podium, medals being placed around necks, national anthem) is shown in these frames.\n\n#### 6) **Gala performances/exhibition skates?**\n- **NO.** All visible performances appear to be **competitive routines** (Short Dance, Free Dance) with score overlays and Olympic branding. There is no evidence of exhibition or gala performances in these frames.\n\n---\n\n### 📌 Conclusion:\n\nThe video is **not** a complete compilation of “every performance at the Olympic Games” by Tessa Virtue and Scott Moir. It is a **highlight reel** featuring **key moments from Vancouver 2010 and Sochi 2014**, but **omits PyeongChang 2018** — their final Olympic Games — entirely from these frames.\n\nThe on-screen text in Frames 5–7 says:  \n> “TESSA VIRTUE AND SCOTT MOIR EVERY PERFORMANCE AT THE OLYMPIC GAMES”\n\nThis is **misleading or inaccurate** based on the visual evidence. The video **does not show every performance**, nor does it include footage from all three Olympic Games they competed in.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n**The answer ‘1’ is incorrect.** The video shows 